<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-Venn-Diagrams" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Venn Diagrams</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> This chapter focuses on Venn Diagrams. What are they? How are they useful? We will use tactiles like dice, wikki-stix, and tactile graph paper to express concepts using Venn Diagrams. </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- include sections -->
  <xi:include href="sec-section-name.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-set-th-notation">
    <title>Some Notation for Set Theory</title>
    
    
    <p>
      <m>e^{i\pi}+1=0</m> Does this work? 
    </p>

<p>
In this section we will be using Venn Diagrams to study Surveys, which is a topic in the area of math called Set Theory.  We need to know a few basic definitions and some notation for our studies.
</p>

<dl>
  <li>
    <title>Sets (or categories)</title>
    <p>
      are represented with capital letters. 
In this example, A and B are the category names.
The Universal Set is represented by the 
rectangular boundary around the sets
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <title>Elements</title>
    <p>
      in a set are sometimes named inside the set.
In this example, Sally, Sam and Solomon are elements.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <title>Regions in a Venn Diagram</title>
    <p>
      in a Venn diagram are sometimes numbered 
with Roman numerals, not to be confused as elements of the set.
    </p>
  </li>
</dl>

<p>
  Sets (or categories) are represented with capital letters. 
In this example, A and B are the category names.
The Universal Set is represented by the 
rectangular boundary around the sets
</p>

<figure xml:id="img-Venn-notation">
  <caption>This image shows the notations used in Venn Diagrams.
    
  </caption>
</figure>

  </section>

</chapter>